# PART J: MAKE SURE JENKINS CAN ACCESS DOCKER AND KUBERNETES

## STEP 37: UNDERSTAND THIS IMPORTANT REQUIREMENT

The pipeline will run commands on the Jenkins agent.

That agent must be able to:

1. Run Docker commands.
2. Push images to Docker Hub.
3. Run kubectl commands.
4. Access the Minikube Kubernetes cluster.

**Commands working in your normal VS Code terminal do not automatically mean they will work in Jenkins.**

Jenkins may run as a Windows service under a different account. That account may not have access to your Docker Desktop connection or your Minikube configuration.

### Check Docker access

On the Windows machine and account configured to run the Jenkins agent, the following command must work:

```powershell
docker --version
```

### Check Kubernetes access

On that same agent, run:

```powershell
kubectl get nodes
```

The Minikube node should show `Ready`.

If either command fails when run by Jenkins, the pipeline will not be able to complete those stages.

You may need to configure the Jenkins service or agent to use the correct Windows account and Kubernetes configuration. Do not expose your credentials while doing so.

