# PART M: TROUBLESHOOTING

## Problem 1: Jenkins does not open

Try visiting:

```text
http://localhost:8080
```

If the page does not open, check whether Jenkins is installed and running.

On Windows, you can press **Windows + R**, type `services.msc`, and press Enter to open Services. Look for the Jenkins service if you installed Jenkins as a Windows service.

If it is stopped, start it. If it fails to start, check the Jenkins installation and Java requirements.

## Problem 2: Docker commands fail

Open Docker Desktop and wait until it is running.

Then try:

```powershell
docker info
```

If this works in VS Code but fails in Jenkins, the Jenkins agent may not have access to Docker Desktop.

## Problem 3: Git commands fail

Check Git:

```powershell
git --version
```

If Git is not recognized, install Git and reopen the terminal.

If Git reports that the remote already exists, check it using:

```powershell
git remote -v
```

## Problem 4: Docker image cannot be uploaded

Check that you have signed in:

```powershell
docker login
```

Then verify the image name and Docker Hub username.

## Problem 5: Minikube does not start

Check that Docker Desktop is running.

Then run:

```powershell
minikube status
```

If Minikube is stopped, try:

```powershell
minikube start --driver=docker
```

Read any error messages before trying further changes.

## Problem 6: Kubernetes cannot find the image

Check the image name in `deployment.yaml`.

It must match the image uploaded to Docker Hub, including the username and tag.

For example:

```yaml
image: YOUR_DOCKERHUB_USERNAME/ai-model-app:latest
```

Replace the placeholder with your actual Docker Hub username.

## Problem 7: Jenkins cannot deploy to Kubernetes

Run this command from the Jenkins agent:

```powershell
kubectl get nodes
```

If it fails, Jenkins may not have access to the Minikube cluster configuration.

Configure access for the Jenkins agent before running the deployment stage again.

